    .system_prompt("You are helpful.")
    .tool(my_tool)
    .build();
```

### ice-tools: Replaceable Tool Implementations

`ice-tools` introduces the `ExecutionEnv` trait, isolating filesystem and shell operations. This means you can use `InMemoryExecutionEnv` in tests and `OsExecutionEnv` in production.

```rust
// ExecutionEnv trait - isolating environment operations
pub trait ExecutionEnv: Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn read_file(&self, path: &Path) -> Result<FileContent, FileError>;
    fn write_file(&self, path: &Path, content: &str) -> Result<(), FileError>;
    fn execute_shell(&self, command: &str, options: ShellExecOptions) -> Result<ShellOutput, ExecutionError>;
}
```

### ice-harness: Persistence and Sessions

`ice-harness` is the heaviest layer, providing session tree, JSONL persistence, context compaction, and more. Only Agents that need durable branching need to import it.

### ice-cli: Command Line Interface

The top layer is `ice-cli`, which combines all the above layers into a complete command-line tool. Importantly, library crates never need to link argparse or TUI code.

## Benefits of This Design

**1. Independent Usage**: If you only need LLM calls, depend only on `ice-ai`; if you need the Agent loop, add `ice-agent`; if you need tools, add `ice-tools`.

**2. Testability**: Each layer can be tested independently. `ice-agent` tests don't need a real filesystem, `ice-tools` tests don't need a real LLM.

